# Start Keycloak/MySQL: docker compose up -d idp_mysql idp_keycloak
# Follow the lecturer's manual setup with the project-specific values below:
# https://gitlab.com/kdg-ti/programming6/material/2026-2027/piggybank-modulith/-/blob/introducing-security/keycloak_setup.md
# Keycloak: http://localhost:8380; realm: airfreight.
# Realm roles: flight-planner, load-planner, admin.
# Users: planner, loader, admin; assign the corresponding role and use local123 for these development fixtures.
# Supply email, first/last name and Email verified; set the password with Temporary off.
# API client: airfreight-api, Client authentication on, Direct access grants on, Standard flow off.
# Keep the profile/email/roles scopes. Add an Audience mapper for airfreight-api to the access token.
# Copy its Credentials > Client secret manually into the ignored src/test/http/http-client.private.env.json:
# {"planner":{"clientSecret":"<secret>"},"loader":{"clientSecret":"<secret>"},"admin":{"clientSecret":"<secret>"}}
# Browser client: airfreight-web, public, Standard flow on, Direct access grants off, PKCE S256.
# Redirect URI: http://localhost:5173/*; web origin: http://localhost:5173; use the same audience mapper.
# Select planner, loader or admin in the IntelliJ HTTP client before running these requests.
# Re-run this request when switching accounts or when the access token expires.

### Obtain a bearer token from Keycloak
POST {{keycloakUrl}}/realms/airfreight/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=password&client_id={{clientId}}&client_secret={{clientSecret}}&username={{username}}&password={{password}}&scope=openid

> {%
    client.test("Keycloak login succeeds", function () { client.assert(response.status === 200); });
    client.global.set("accessToken", response.body.access_token);
    client.global.set("flightDate", new Date(Date.now() - 86400000).toISOString().slice(0, 10));
%}
